# docs-site

Generated documentation for the __APP_NAME__ contract. Every page on this
site is read from the protos at build time; none of it is hand-written.

## Why this exists

It is the worked example of a site that uses the contract as **content**.
The page is static — nginx serving files, no server, no runtime fetch — and
protobuf is what decided what it displays. That combination is why sites sit
on the client limb of the stack rather than off to one side.

The property worth having is not that the docs are automatic. It is that
they *cannot* be wrong: if a resource loses a field, this site does not
quietly keep describing it, because there is nothing here to keep. The build
regenerates from the same descriptor the backend and the interface generate
from.

## How it works

```
proto/            hand-written protos
   │  buf build --as-file-descriptor-set
   ▼
src/generated/descriptor.json      (gitignored, regenerated)
   │  src/contract.ts walks it
   ▼
src/pages/*.astro                   renders services, RPCs, messages, fields
```

`src/contract.ts` reads the descriptor set rather than the generated
TypeScript for one reason: proto comments survive into generated code only as
JSDoc, which nothing can read at runtime. `buf build` keeps them in
SourceCodeInfo. That is what makes the comments in your `.proto` files
load-bearing — they are the prose on these pages.

## Running it

From the repo root:

```bash
pnpm dev:docs-site      # regenerates the descriptor, then serves
pnpm build:docs-site    # static files into packages/docs-site/dist
```

The descriptor is regenerated first by both scripts, so editing a proto and
refreshing is the whole loop.

## Making it yours

This is a teaching specimen, like the `Note` resource. Some directions it
usually grows in:

- **Group by resource** rather than listing every message flat, once the
  contract has more than a handful.
- **Show examples that compile.** Sample client code written against the
  generated client and typechecked with the site cannot go stale, which is
  the same guarantee the field tables have.
- **Render enums and nested messages**, which `contract.ts` currently skips
  in favour of staying readable.
- **Give it a real design.** The CSS here is deliberately plain; inherit the
  family design language when this becomes a surface people are sent to.

If you would rather not publish your contract at all, delete the package —
nothing else depends on it.
